  {
   "id": "abd-body",
   "type": "text",
   "x": 3370.0,
   "y": 254,
   "width": 549.0,
   "height": 468.75,
   "angle": 0,
   "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
   "backgroundColor": "transparent",
   "fillStyle": "solid",
   "strokeWidth": 2,
   "strokeStyle": "solid",
   "roughness": 0,
   "opacity": 100,
   "groupIds": [],
   "frameId": "frame-dt",
   "roundness": null,
   "seed": 348822933,
   "version": 1,
   "versionNonce": 1122719968,
   "isDeleted": false,
   "boundElements": [],
   "updated": 1790826922400,
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "text": "              you know         you look for\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nDeduction     what + how       the result         predict\nInduction     what + result    the how            explain\nAbduction     result + how     the what           choose\nDesign        the result       the what AND       create\n              (the value)      the how            both\n\nDorst 2011, after Peirce.\n\nInsight works on the second row: from what was sent and what\nhappened, explain how it worked.\n\nPicking the best of a fixed set is the third row: selection,\nnot design. Round 1 was run that way: 1 of 13 written texts.\n\nDesign is the last row. Only the value is known (more\npatients review their prescription); neither the artifact nor\nthe principle that would make it work is. There is no option\nset to search: the designer frames a principle, then makes\noptions from it.\n\nSimon 1969: design changes an existing situation into a\npreferred one. It satisfices: good enough within the\nconstraints, never provably best.",
   "originalText": "              you know         you look for\n───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nDeduction     what + how       the result         predict\nInduction     what + result    the how            explain\nAbduction     result + how     the what           choose\nDesign        the result       the what AND       create\n              (the value)      the how            both\n\nDorst 2011, after Peirce.\n\nInsight works on the second row: from what was sent and what\nhappened, explain how it worked.\n\nPicking the best of a fixed set is the third row: selection,\nnot design. Round 1 was run that way: 1 of 13 written texts.\n\nDesign is the last row. Only the value is known (more\npatients review their prescription); neither the artifact nor\nthe principle that would make it work is. There is no option\nset to search: the designer frames a principle, then makes\noptions from it.\n\nSimon 1969: design changes an existing situation into a\npreferred one. It satisfices: good enough within the\nconstraints, never provably best.",
   "fontSize": 15,
   "fontFamily": 3,
   "textAlign": "left",
   "verticalAlign": "top",
   "containerId": null,
   "autoResize": true,
   "lineHeight": 1.25
  },
  {
   "id": "abd-foot",
   "type": "text",
   "x": 3370.0,
   "y": 820,
   "width": 448.00000000000006,
   "height": 40.0,
   "angle": 0,
   "strokeColor": "#1864ab",
   "backgroundColor": "transparent",
   "fillStyle": "solid",
   "strokeWidth": 2,
   "strokeStyle": "solid",
   "roughness": 0,
   "opacity": 100,
   "groupIds": [],
   "frameId": "frame-dt",
   "roundness": null,
   "seed": 911288330,
   "version": 1,
   "versionNonce": 1657106232,
   "isDeleted": false,
   "boundElements": [],
   "updated": 1790826922400,
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "text": "Choosing among options is the easy part.\nThe work is creating options worth choosing among.",
   "originalText": "Choosing among options is the easy part.\nThe work is creating options worth choosing among.",
   "fontSize": 16,
   "fontFamily": 8,
   "textAlign": "left",
   "verticalAlign": "top",
   "containerId": null,
   "autoResize": true,
   "lineHeight": 1.25
  },
